&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransport+send+groups+and+send%5C-stream+prioritization%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;[blink-dev] Re: Intent to Ship: WebTransport send groups and send-stream prioritization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22%27Viktoriya+Bryhider%27+via+blink%5C-dev%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;'Viktoriya Bryhider' via blink-dev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261030" rel="nofollow"&gt;Fri, 30 Oct 2026 06:54:55 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;
This feature implementation provides the scheduling functionality for 
sendGroup and  sendOrder, which will also help enable 
WebTransportDatagramsWritable -  WebTransport Datagram writable streams and 
prioritization - Chrome Platform Status 
&lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5183354944225280"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5183354944225280&lt;/a&gt;&gt;.&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

On Wednesday, October 28, 2026 at 4:34:36 PM UTC-7 Chromestatus wrote:

&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="ef998d9d96c1c1c1af82868c9d809c80899bc18c8082"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#6-sending-video-one-stream-per-frame-or-segment-with-send-order"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#6-sending-video-one-stream-per-frame-or-segment-with-send-order&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#7-sending-real-time-game-data-per-stream-with-send-order"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#7-sending-real-time-game-data-per-stream-with-send-order&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#8-managing-bandwidth-with-send-groups"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md#8-managing-bandwidth-with-send-groups&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Specification*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#webtransportsendstream"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#webtransportsendstream&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;
&gt; *Summary*
&gt; WebTransport already lets sites send data through WritableStreams. This 
&gt; feature lets applications assign outgoing streams to send groups and use 
&gt; sendOrder to prioritize streams within each group. It also provides sending 
&gt; statistics for streams and groups while preserving WritableStream transfer 
&gt; behavior. 
&gt;
&gt; *Blink component*
&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;
&gt; *Motivation*
&gt; Real-time applications often send multiple independent flows over one 
&gt; WebTransport session. When those flows compete for bandwidth, applications 
&gt; need to keep one flow from dominating while deciding which streams within 
&gt; each flow should go first. They also need visibility into sending progress 
&gt; so they can understand how those flows are performing. 
&gt;
&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;
&gt; *TAG review*
&gt; *No information provided* 
&gt;
&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt; Not applicable
&gt;
&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt; None 
&gt;
&gt; *Risks*
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt; Firefox and Safari already expose WebTransportSendStream and the 
&gt; WebTransportSendGroup API; send groups shipped in Firefox 155 and Safari 
&gt; 26.4. Changing Chrome’s outgoing streams from plain WritableStream objects 
&gt; to WebTransportSendStream objects therefore aligns their JavaScript type 
&gt; with those browsers. Existing writing behavior and instanceof 
&gt; WritableStream checks remain compatible. Enabling this feature changes 
&gt; outgoing unidirectional streams and the writable side of bidirectional 
&gt; streams from plain WritableStream objects to WebTransportSendStream 
&gt; objects, so exact constructor or prototype check may need updating. 
&gt;
&gt; *Gecko*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2007165"&gt;https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2007165&lt;/a&gt;) Firefox exposes 
&gt;  WebTransportSendStream as a  WritableStream  subclass for outgoing 
&gt; WebTransport streams, including streams created by 
&gt; createUnidirectionalStream(). It added WebTransportSendGroup and 
&gt; createSendGroup() in Firefox 155, released September 1, 2026; group 
&gt; assignments are passed to its network implementation - 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.firefox.com/en-US/firefox/155.0/releasenotes/"&gt;https://www.firefox.com/en-US/firefox/155.0/releasenotes/&lt;/a&gt; Firefox does 
&gt; not yet provide equivalent statistics behavior. In its current source, 
&gt; WebTransportSendStream.getStats() rejects with NotSupportedError.
&gt;
&gt; *WebKit*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/blob/main/api/WebTransportSendGroup.json#L52"&gt;https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/blob/main/api/WebTransportSendGroup.json#L52&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;  WebKit 
&gt; implements WebTransportSendGroup, WebTransportSendStream, 
&gt; sendGroup/sendOrder, both statistics methods, transferability, and the 
&gt; specialized writer. Safari 26.4 includes these WebTransport additions.
&gt;
&gt; *Web developers*: Positive (
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121&lt;/a&gt;) The Interop 
&gt; 2026 WebTransport proposal received 28 positive reactions. Developers also 
&gt; reported active WebTransport use and significant cross-engine differences.
&gt;
&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;
&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt; Send groups divide outgoing streams into separate bandwidth-sharing 
&gt; groups; sendOrder prioritizes streams within a group, groups are scheduled 
&gt; separately. Developers could mistake a higher sendOrder for a guarantee of 
&gt; delivery order or throughput. Priority is sender-side scheduling, and flow 
&gt; control and network conditions still affect delivery. Stream and group 
&gt; getStats() return asynchronous snapshots; bytesWritten, bytesSent, and 
&gt; bytesAcknowledged represent different stages of transmission, not 
&gt; interchangeable totals. Outgoing streams remain WritableStream subclasses, 
&gt; so existing writing APIs continue to work. The specialized 
&gt; WebTransportWriter is not part of this launch: getWriter() still returns a 
&gt; WritableStreamDefaultWriter.
&gt;
&gt; *Activation*
&gt; Enabling the feature changes the runtime type and prototype of outgoing 
&gt; unidirectional streams and the writable side of bidirectional streams from 
&gt; plain WritableStream to WebTransportSendStream for all sites using those 
&gt; streams; sites do not need to call createSendGroup() to observe that 
&gt; change. Code that checks the exact constructor or prototype may behave 
&gt; differently if code assumes exact stream type: stream.constructor === 
&gt; WritableStream  or Object.getPrototypeOf(stream) === 
&gt; WritableStream.prototype. Existing stream writes should continue to work, 
&gt; and no server change is needed to use the new client-side priority 
&gt; controls. Transfer of existing send streams must continue working before 
&gt; activation.
&gt;
&gt; *Security*
&gt; No new network capability or origin boundary is introduced. The API 
&gt; operates only on streams belonging to an existing secure-context 
&gt; WebTransport session. A send group from another WebTransport object is 
&gt; rejected. Statistics expose byte counters for the application's own streams 
&gt; and groups.
&gt;
&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;
&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that 
&gt; it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? 
&gt; Low. This is an additive API on existing WebTransport streams and does not 
&gt; change connection establishment or network behavior. 
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt; The stream's sendGroup and sendOrder values are directly observable from 
&gt; script. Both stream and group getStats() expose bytesWritten, bytesSent, 
&gt; and bytesAcknowledged; group statistics aggregate streams in that group. 
&gt; DevTools does not currently provide dedicated WebTransport stream 
&gt; inspection, so developers primarily diagnose this API through script and 
&gt; application logging. 
&gt;
&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, 
&gt; Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt; Yes
&gt;
&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt; Yes 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendgroup.https.any.html"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendgroup.https.any.html&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendorder.https.any.html"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendorder.https.any.html&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendstream-bad-chunk.https.any.html"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/sendstream-bad-chunk.https.any.html&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/send-stream-stats.https.any.html"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/send-stream-stats.https.any.html&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt; enable-experimental-web-platform-features 
&gt;
&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt; WebTransportSendGroup 
&gt;
&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;
&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt; False
&gt;
&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://crbug.com/568825278"&gt;https://crbug.com/568825278&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; *Availability expectation*
&gt; Firefox 155 ships createSendGroup(), and Safari 26.4 ships WebTransport 
&gt; with the corresponding WebKit group and send-stream interfaces. Chrome 
&gt; shipping would make the API shape available across the major engines.
&gt;
&gt; *Adoption expectation*
&gt; Adoption is expected mainly among existing WebTransport applications with 
&gt; multiple concurrent streams and latency-sensitive traffic.
&gt;
&gt; *Adoption plan*
&gt; Adoption is expected to occur organically through WebTransport 
&gt; documentation, examples, and client library support. Developers can 
&gt; progressively enhance existing code by feature-detecting getStats().
&gt;
&gt; *Non-OSS dependencies*
&gt;
&gt; Does the feature depend on any code or APIs outside the Chromium open 
&gt; source repository and its open-source dependencies to function? 
&gt; No
&gt;
&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt; Shipping on desktop 159 
&gt; DevTrial on desktop 153 
&gt; Shipping on Android 159 
&gt; DevTrial on Android 153 
&gt; Shipping on WebView 159 
&gt;
&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;
&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or 
&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues 
&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may 
&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure of 
&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). 
&gt; None known for the WebTransportSendStream or WebTransportSendGroup.
&gt;
&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5235261159112704?gate=6451678743232512"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5235261159112704?gate=6451678743232512&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. 
&gt;

-- 
