&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Extend+Experiment%5C%3A+WebMCP%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Re: Intent to Extend Experiment: WebMCP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22%27Yashaswini+Kumar%27+via+blink%5C-dev%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;'Yashaswini Kumar' via blink-dev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261102" rel="nofollow"&gt;Mon, 02 Nov 2026 12:54:25 -0800&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;Hi Rick,

We have: ModelContextExecuteTool
&lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5943"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5943&lt;/a&gt;&gt;,
ModelContextExecuteDeclarativeTool
&lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5800"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5800&lt;/a&gt;&gt;,
ModelContextExecuteImperativeTool
&lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5799"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5799&lt;/a&gt;&gt; and
ModelContextGetTools
&lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5904"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/timeline/popularity/5904&lt;/a&gt;&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

Thanks,
Yash

On Mon, Nov 2, 2026 at 11:04 AM Rick Byers &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="3e4c5c471010107e5d564c5153574b5310514c59"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:

&gt; LGTM2. Seems like a metrics bug to me, I'd be happy to use actual tool
&gt; invocation as the metric that matters here (that's where the burn-in risk
&gt; would come from). Is there a public UseCounter for that already?
&gt;
&gt; Rick
&gt;
&gt; On Wed, Oct 28, 2026 at 11:59 AM Mike Taylor &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="4e2327252b3a2f6060600e2d263c2123273b2360213c29"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt; wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; LGTM1, given the &quot;actual invocation&quot; info below. Thanks.
&gt;&gt; On 10/28/26 2:55 p.m., 'Yashaswini Kumar' via blink-dev wrote:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Hi blink-dev and API Owners,
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; We would like to request an Origin Trial usage limit exception for the
&gt;&gt; WebMCP experiment to allow page load usage to exceed the 0.5% warning
&gt;&gt; threshold up to a maximum of 2.0%.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; ### Context
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; WebMCP's Origin Trial registration usage has recently crossed the warning
&gt;&gt; threshold, currently sitting at approximately 0.51% of all Chrome page
&gt;&gt; loads. Under typical Origin Trial guidance, reaching 0.5% warrants either
&gt;&gt; asking partners to throttle usage or requesting an exception from API
&gt;&gt; owners before approaching the 1.0% trial limit.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; ### Justification &amp; Burn-in Risk Assessment
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; We believe extending the threshold to 2.0% carries negligible web burn-in
&gt;&gt; risk for the following reasons:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; 1. Token Registrations vs. Actual Invocation:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    The ~0.51% metric reflects pages where WebMCP tools are registered via
&gt;&gt; token. However, actual runtime invocation and execution of WebMCP tools by
&gt;&gt; AI agents is orders of magnitude lower than the page registration rate.
&gt;&gt; Site users do not actively execute tool calls on every visit.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; 2. Plateaued Growth:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    Partner registrations have largely stabilized and plateaued over the
&gt;&gt; past several months, indicating steady experimentation rather than runaway
&gt;&gt; exponential growth across the broader web.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; 3. Continued Operational Monitoring:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    The WebMCP team actively monitors partner usage and retains tight
&gt;&gt; operational controls, ensuring we can intervene well before usage
&gt;&gt; approaches 2.0%.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; ### Request
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; We request 3 LGTMs from the Blink API Owners to grant an exception
&gt;&gt; allowing WebMCP Origin Trial usage up to 2.0% of Chrome page loads.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thanks,
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Yash on behalf of the Chrome WebMCP team
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; On Tuesday, September 29, 2026 at 2:47:43 PM UTC-4 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="04602a2a2a44676c766b696d71692a6b7663"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; Hey Alex, yeah, we’re hoping to move to I2S within the extension window.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; So far we’ve learned a lot:
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; The imperative API is a lot more popular than the declarative one, so
&gt;&gt;&gt; we're investing more into it in response to user needs.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    1.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    We received excellent feedback from Mozilla
&gt;&gt;&gt;    &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/236"&gt;https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/236&lt;/a&gt;&gt; about API
&gt;&gt;&gt;    changes we can make to benefit non-AI use cases, as a generic cross-realm
&gt;&gt;&gt;    function-calling primitive.
&gt;&gt;&gt;    2.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    We've heard from partners with tools whose entrypoint is on one
&gt;&gt;&gt;    page, while the result is on another (after a navigation). So we're
&gt;&gt;&gt;    prototyping a continuation API
&gt;&gt;&gt;    
&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/blob/cont-explainer/continuations-explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/blob/cont-explainer/continuations-explainer.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    that we'd like to get into the hands of developers.
&gt;&gt;&gt;    3.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    We and popular frameworks anticipate gaps between async tool
&gt;&gt;&gt;    registration and when an agent collects a &quot;snapshot&quot; of a page's tools
&gt;&gt;&gt;    (driven by its page loading heuristics). So we're prototyping a tool
&gt;&gt;&gt;    stability API
&gt;&gt;&gt;    &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/328"&gt;https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/328&lt;/a&gt;&gt; to let
&gt;&gt;&gt;    developers inform agent heuristics when an appropriate observation time
&gt;&gt;&gt;    might be. We're discussing this with major model vendors and want to
&gt;&gt;&gt;    understand how this impacts performance in the real world.
&gt;&gt;&gt;    4.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    Some developers expressed a desire to pass user activation from an
&gt;&gt;&gt;    in-page agent (on a top-level document), into the page hosting a a
&gt;&gt;&gt;    tool. We're tracking this in
&gt;&gt;&gt;    &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/62"&gt;https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/62&lt;/a&gt;, but before
&gt;&gt;&gt;    pursuing a proposal like capability delegation
&gt;&gt;&gt;    &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WICG/capability-delegation"&gt;https://github.com/WICG/capability-delegation&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, we'd like more
&gt;&gt;&gt;    experimentation to help us understand how much this comes up in practice.
&gt;&gt;&gt;    5.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;    We're exploring what it might look like to enforce size/character
&gt;&gt;&gt;    limits on tools
&gt;&gt;&gt;    &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/219"&gt;https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp/issues/219&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, and we'd
&gt;&gt;&gt;    benefit from more time to experiment after discussing what a reasonable
&gt;&gt;&gt;    limit might look like with other browsers and major model vendors.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; Those are a few things we've learned so far, and in particular, how we'd
&gt;&gt;&gt; benefit from an extended experiment to understand how our proposed changes
&gt;&gt;&gt; play with real-world sites and agents.
&gt;&gt;&gt; On Monday, September 28, 2026 at 7:57:08 PM UTC-4 Alex Russell wrote:
