&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Clarification+on+Origin+Trial+Usage+Limits+and+Token+Distribution%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Clarification on Origin Trial Usage Limits and Token Distribution&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Ian+Clelland%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Ian Clelland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261103" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 03 Nov 2026 06:51:20 -0800&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;Hi Vibhuti -

I haven't been involved in the origin trials program for quite some time
now, but I don't think the criteria has changed - usage is based on feature
use, not on the presence or absence of the token in headers. It should be
fine, for most trials, to ship the header on every page view, as long as
you're not triggering the feature on every page load. We count the
number of page loads where the feature is used at least once.&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

That said, the 1% limit applies across *all* page loads measured by Chrome;
generally you would have to be an extremely large popular site to trigger
that by yourself. A very large number of smaller sites could potentially
cause the trial to reach this limit as well, though that is less likely for
an origin trial, given that each site has to sign up for their own token.

A rough but mostly-correct proxy for this calculation is the feature
popularity metrics we publish at
&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity&lt;/a&gt; - if that number were
to climb above 1% for a feature while it was still in an origin trial, that
would likely trigger the usage limits.

This is mostly documented in
&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/GoogleChrome/OriginTrials/blob/gh-pages/developer-guide.md#19-are-there-any-usage-limits-on-experimental-features"&gt;https://github.com/GoogleChrome/OriginTrials/blob/gh-pages/developer-guide.md#19-are-there-any-usage-limits-on-experimental-features&lt;/a&gt;

Hope this helps,
Ian

