&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Experiment%5C%3A+Opening+links+in+a+split+tab%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Experiment: Opening links in a split tab&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Mason+Freed%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Mason Freed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261110" rel="nofollow"&gt;Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:36:44 -0800&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;On Tue, Nov 10, 2026 at 10:23 AM Yoav Weiss (@Shopify) &lt;
&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="196076786f6e7c373737597a716b7674706c7437766b7e"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

&gt; I talk a bit about feature detection in this section of the explainer
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mfreed7/split-tabs#feature-detection-navigatorsplittabsupported"&gt;https://github.com/mfreed7/split-tabs#feature-detection-navigatorsplittabsupported&lt;/a&gt;&gt;.
&gt;&gt; The short answer is that you can detect support for the API itself via
&gt;&gt; `&quot;windowFeatures&quot; in HTMLAnchorelement` or similar, but you can't (for
&gt;&gt; privacy reasons) detect a-priori whether you'll actually *get* a split tab.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;
&gt; Interesting! What are the privacy reasons? The &quot;If the window is already
&gt; split&quot; case? Something else?
&gt;

I'd be very happy to be told I'm wrong, but the concern is that it would
expose whether your system *can* open a split tab (e.g. mobile vs.
desktop), and whether you already have a split tab open in the same tab. I
think the concern about this is actually fairly small, since those likely
don't add meaningful entropy (if any) to what's already available. But
that's the concern. I'd love to expose this bit, since I'm guessing it'll
be helpful when actually using the API to know whether you got a split tab
or not.

Thanks,
Mason



&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thanks,
&gt;&gt; Mason
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Thanks,
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Mason
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
