&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+Prompt+API+Sampling+Mode%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: Prompt API Sampling Mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Jeffrey+Yasskin%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Jeffrey Yasskin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261109" rel="nofollow"&gt;Mon, 09 Nov 2026 10:43:16 -0800&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;On Mon, Nov 9, 2026 at 10:24 AM Chromestatus &lt;
&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="5a3b3e7474741a392877292e3b2e2f29743b2a2a292a352e373b333674393537"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

&gt; *TAG review*
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1093"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1093&lt;/a&gt; This update directly
&gt; addresses the TAG’s Prompt API early design review feedback on
&gt; “Interoperable model parameters”. We adopted TAG guidance by replacing raw
&gt; hyperparameters with samplingMode presets, which enable user agents to map
&gt; semantic developer intent to model-specific sampling configurations.
&gt; Because this incremental refinement directly fulfills prior TAG advice
&gt; rather than a new architectural surface, a separate review is not merited.
&gt;

Even though you're implementing a goal the TAG expressed, the TAG may still
have feedback on _how_ you're implementing it. I can't promise that we'll
give timely feedback, but I think it's still worth sending a review for
this.

Thanks,
Jeffrey

-- 
