&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mfreed7/split-tabs"&gt;https://github.com/mfreed7/split-tabs&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Specification*
&gt;&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Summary*
&gt;&gt; Split screen scenarios are increasingly common. In addition to dual
&gt;&gt; screen devices (i.e. physical split screen), many browsers have a split tab
&gt;&gt; (i.e. virtual split screen) feature, where two tabs are tiled side-by-side
&gt;&gt; within a single browser window. When using a split tab, there are multiple
&gt;&gt; cases in which users want to open a link and view it side-by-side with the
&gt;&gt; source page. Today there is no way for a web author to request that a
&gt;&gt; navigation open into a split tab. The existing navigation targets (_blank,
&gt;&gt; _self, _parent, _top) cannot express this, and windowFeatures only offers
&gt;&gt; popup, which opens a separate, detached browser window. Without popup,
&gt;&gt; window.open() opens the content as a new full-size tab in the current
&gt;&gt; window, which is the only other option available. This feature provides
&gt;&gt; methods (imperative and declarative) for web authors to request that a link
&gt;&gt; open in a split tab, alongside the current page.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Blink component*
&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;DOM
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EDOM%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3EDOM%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt;&gt; Missing feature
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Search tags*
&gt;&gt; window.open &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/features#tags:window.open"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/features#tags:window.open&lt;/a&gt;&gt;, split
&gt;&gt; tab &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/features#tags:split%20tab"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/features#tags:split%20tab&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1309"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1309&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt;&gt; Pending
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt;&gt; None
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Risks*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt;&gt; There should be no compat risks, since these are new window feature
&gt;&gt; values and a new attribute. (Extremely small, and acceptable, risk that
&gt;&gt; existing content already uses the `windowFeatures` attribute.) The API also
&gt;&gt; includes provisions for feature detection and easy progressive enhancement,
&gt;&gt; such as the ability to explicitly fall back to popup behavior.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: No signal (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1481"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/1481&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: No signal (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/741"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/741&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive Private feedback is quite positive.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;&gt;
