&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Re%5C%3A+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransportError+API+alignment+and+serialization%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Re: Intent to Ship: WebTransportError API alignment and serialization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Alex+Russell%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Alex Russell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261104" rel="nofollow"&gt;Wed, 04 Nov 2026 09:19:04 -0800&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;LGTM3

On Wednesday, November 4, 2026 at 8:33:07 AM UTC-8 Daniel Bratell wrote:&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

&gt; Or make that LGTM2
&gt;
&gt; /Daniel
&gt; On 2026-11-04 17:31, Daniel Bratell wrote:
&gt;
&gt; LGTM1
&gt;
&gt; /Daniel
&gt;
&gt; On Tuesday, 3 November 2026 at 23:21:22 UTC+1 Chromestatus wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="cfa2a0a1a6e1e1e18fa2a6acbda0bca0a9bbe1aca0a2"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Specification*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#web-transport-error-interface"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#web-transport-error-interface&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Design docs*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransportError"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransportError&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/web-apis/webtransport"&gt;https://developer.chrome.com/docs/capabilities/web-apis/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Summary*
&gt;&gt; Updates Chromium WebTransportError to match the current WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; specification. Adds the standard (message, options) constructor while 
&gt;&gt; preserving the shipped dictionary constructor for compatibility; carries 
&gt;&gt; the full unsigned-long stream error code end to end; and makes 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportError structured-clonable while preserving its message, source, 
&gt;&gt; and nullable error code. Serialization is enabled by default behind a 
&gt;&gt; writer-side runtime kill switch, while decoding remains permanently enabled 
&gt;&gt; for persisted values. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Blink component*
&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt;&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Motivation*
&gt;&gt; Chromium's shipped WebTransportError implementation predates the current 
&gt;&gt; WebTransport specification. It accepts a legacy dictionary-only 
&gt;&gt; constructor, narrows outgoing application stream error codes to 8 bits 
&gt;&gt; despite the API exposing an unsigned long, and does not support the 
&gt;&gt; structured serialization required by the specification. The change aligns 
&gt;&gt; Chromium with the standardized API and improves WebTransport 
&gt;&gt; interoperability. The legacy constructor remains available so existing 
&gt;&gt; Chromium applications continue to work. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt;&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/669"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/669&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt;&gt; Not applicable
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt;&gt; None 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Risks*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt;&gt; Interoperability risk Low. The constructor, WebTransportErrorOptions 
&gt;&gt; dictionary, unsigned-long streamErrorCode, source attribute, and 
&gt;&gt; serialization behavior are defined in the W3C WebTransport specification 
&gt;&gt; and covered by Web Platform Tests. Mozilla implemented the same constructor 
&gt;&gt; and full-width stream error-code behavior and contributed the relevant WPT 
&gt;&gt; coverage: &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/63842"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/63842&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt; Compatibility risk Low. Chromium's shipped dictionary constructor is 
&gt;&gt; retained as a compatibility overload, including its existing octet clamping 
&gt;&gt; and handling of trailing arguments. Existing calls therefore continue to 
&gt;&gt; work. The intentional behavior changes are that application stream error 
&gt;&gt; codes above 255 are no longer truncated, and structured-cloning a 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportError now succeeds instead of throwing DataCloneError. Code 
&gt;&gt; that deliberately depended on either non-standard behavior could observe a 
&gt;&gt; difference. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2009530"&gt;https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2009530&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=305224"&gt;https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=305224&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive (
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121&lt;/a&gt;) The Interop 
&gt;&gt; 2026 WebTransport proposal received 28 👍 reactions. Developers also 
&gt;&gt; reported active WebTransport use and significant cross-engine differences.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt;&gt; Low. The standard constructor follows familiar Error and DOMException 
&gt;&gt; patterns: new WebTransportError(&quot;message&quot;, { source: &quot;stream&quot;, 
&gt;&gt; streamErrorCode: 70000 }); Existing Chromium dictionary-style construction 
&gt;&gt; remains supported. Structured cloning preserves the WebTransportError 
&gt;&gt; subtype and its WebTransport-specific properties instead of reducing it to 
&gt;&gt; a generic exception or rejecting it.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Activation*
&gt;&gt; Low. The standard constructor and full-width stream error-code support 
&gt;&gt; correct existing non-conforming behavior while preserving Chromium's legacy 
&gt;&gt; constructor. Structured serialization is newly observable behavior. It is 
&gt;&gt; controlled by the WebTransportErrorSerialization runtime feature, which is 
&gt;&gt; enabled by default but retained as a writer-side kill switch. The 
&gt;&gt; deserializer is intentionally always enabled so WebTransportError values 
&gt;&gt; already stored in IndexedDB or other structured-clone storage remain 
&gt;&gt; readable if writing is disabled. The implementation is covered by focused 
&gt;&gt; Blink and Network Service tests and the Mozilla Web Platform Tests.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Security*
&gt;&gt; Low. The change does not grant additional network access, expose new 
&gt;&gt; server or user information, or weaken WebTransport's secure-context 
&gt;&gt; requirement. Structured cloning copies only the WebTransportError subtype, 
&gt;&gt; DOMString message, source enum, and nullable unsigned 32-bit stream error 
&gt;&gt; code. The versioned decoder validates the format version, enum 
&gt;&gt; discriminants, presence flags, string code units, and payload completeness 
&gt;&gt; before constructing an object.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that 
&gt;&gt; it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? 
&gt;&gt; Low risk. This aligns the existing WebTransportError API with the current 
&gt;&gt; specification. Existing error values from 0–255 retain identical behavior, 
&gt;&gt; the legacy constructor remains supported, and ordinary creation, throwing, 
&gt;&gt; and catching are unchanged. Structured cloning changes from throwing 
&gt;&gt; DataCloneError to succeeding. Serialization writes are protected by a kill 
&gt;&gt; switch, while the reader remains enabled for compatibility with previously 
&gt;&gt; persisted data. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt;&gt; No feature-specific DevTools support is required. Developers can inspect 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportError.name, message, source, and streamErrorCode in the 
&gt;&gt; DevTools Console. Existing WebTransport diagnostics, NetLog, Network 
&gt;&gt; Service logging, and QUIC logging remain available for tracing the 
&gt;&gt; underlying stream error. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, 
&gt;&gt; Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt;&gt; Yes
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt;&gt; Yes 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/idlharness.https.sub.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *DevTrial instructions*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransportError#examples"&gt;https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransportError#examples&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt;&gt; enable-experimental-web-platform-features 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt;&gt; WebTransportErrorSerialization 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Non-finch justification*
&gt;&gt; Ships enabled to 100%. The runtime feature is retained as a serialization 
&gt;&gt; kill switch, not for a gradual Finch experiment.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt;&gt; False
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/577073049"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/577073049&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Measurement*
&gt;&gt; A Blink UseCounter will measure use of Chromium's legacy dictionary 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportError constructor so that its compatibility impact can be 
&gt;&gt; evaluated before any future deprecation. The standardized constructor, 
&gt;&gt; uint32 stream error-code propagation, and serialization behavior are 
&gt;&gt; validated through WPT and Chromium integration tests. No dedicated UMA 
&gt;&gt; metric is planned for serialization because it is a deterministic 
&gt;&gt; structured-clone capability and does not change network policy or resource 
&gt;&gt; allocation.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Availability expectation*
&gt;&gt; Firefox has implemented the aligned WebTransportError API, including 
&gt;&gt; structured serialization. WebKit supports WebTransport and has landed the 
&gt;&gt; updated constructor and stream error-code behavior, although 
&gt;&gt; WebTransportError-specific structured serialization has not yet been 
&gt;&gt; confirmed. Remaining interoperability is being tracked through Interop 2026.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation*
&gt;&gt; Existing WebTransport applications benefit automatically from the 
&gt;&gt; corrected constructor and full-width stream error codes. Applications using 
&gt;&gt; workers, postMessage(), structuredClone(), or persistent structured-clone 
&gt;&gt; storage can propagate WebTransportError values without custom conversion. 
&gt;&gt; No migration is required for existing users
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Adoption plan*
&gt;&gt; Ship the standards-aligned behavior by default, maintain Web Platform 
&gt;&gt; Test coverage, and track cross-browser implementation through the Interop 
&gt;&gt; 2026 WebTransport effort. This is primarily a compatibility and 
&gt;&gt; interoperability correction, so no separate developer migration campaign is 
&gt;&gt; necessary.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 159 
&gt;&gt; DevTrial on desktop 159 
&gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 159 
&gt;&gt; DevTrial on Android 159 
&gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 159 
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or 
&gt;&gt; interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github issues 
&gt;&gt; in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may 
&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure of 
&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). 
&gt;&gt; None anticipated. This implements the current WebTransport specification 
&gt;&gt; rather than introducing a new API design. No known open specification 
&gt;&gt; issues are expected to require incompatible changes to this API.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5158277221580800?gate=5546593351761920"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5158277221580800?gate=5546593351761920&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status 
&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. 
&gt;&gt;
&gt; -- 
