On Tue, 3 Nov 2026 at 20:20, Ian Clelland &lt;&lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="167f757a737a7a38383856757e64797b7f637b38796471"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:

&gt; Hi Vibhuti -
&gt;
&gt; I haven't been involved in the origin trials program for quite some time
&gt; now, but I don't think the criteria has changed - usage is based on feature
&gt; use, not on the presence or absence of the token in headers. It should be
&gt; fine, for most trials, to ship the header on every page view, as long as
&gt; you're not triggering the feature on every page load. We count the
&gt; number of page loads where the feature is used at least once.
&gt;
&gt; That said, the 1% limit applies across *all* page loads measured by
&gt; Chrome; generally you would have to be an extremely large popular site to
&gt; trigger that by yourself. A very large number of smaller sites could
&gt; potentially cause the trial to reach this limit as well, though that is
&gt; less likely for an origin trial, given that each site has to sign up for
&gt; their own token.
&gt;
&gt; A rough but mostly-correct proxy for this calculation is the feature
&gt; popularity metrics we publish at
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity&lt;/a&gt; - if that number were
&gt; to climb above 1% for a feature while it was still in an origin trial, that
&gt; would likely trigger the usage limits.
&gt;
&gt; This is mostly documented in
&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/GoogleChrome/OriginTrials/blob/gh-pages/developer-guide.md#19-are-there-any-usage-limits-on-experimental-features"&gt;https://github.com/GoogleChrome/OriginTrials/blob/gh-pages/developer-guide.md#19-are-there-any-usage-limits-on-experimental-features&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;
&gt; Hope this helps,
&gt; Ian
&gt;
&gt; On Tue, Oct 20, 2026 at 11:57 AM Vibhuti Mahajan &lt;
&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="186e717a706d6c71757970797279363636587f75797174367b7775"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; Hi Blink Dev Community,
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; We're planning to enable an Origin Trial for our web application and
&gt;&gt; would appreciate clarification on how the usage restrictions are enforced.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; We have two questions:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    1.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    *What counts toward the usage limit?* Is usage measured based on
&gt;&gt;    pages receiving a valid Origin-Trial response header, or only when
&gt;&gt;    the experimental API is actually invoked? Specifically, can we send the
&gt;&gt;    token to all users but restrict actual feature usage to a small subset, or
&gt;&gt;    should we limit token distribution itself?
&gt;&gt;    2.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;    *How is the usage percentage calculated?* Our trial disclosure
&gt;&gt;    mentions a 1% usage limit. Is this calculated against our application's
&gt;&gt;    traffic, or against total Chrome page loads globally?
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Any clarification or pointers to relevant documentation would be greatly
&gt;&gt; appreciated.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thanks!
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; --
