&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt; Agree with Mike that we should not extrapolate and should, instead,
&gt;&gt;&gt; file requests to get them on the record. Even strong opposition won't block
&gt;&gt;&gt; us shipping, so there's no risk here of filing
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; Mozilla is already shipping this so we can change Gecko chrome status
&gt;&gt;&gt; to &quot;shipping&quot;.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; &gt; As for the specific API, it's good to see, but I can't help wonder
&gt;&gt;&gt; when this will be available from Workers. It's the highest priority request
&gt;&gt;&gt; about this API that we hear from teams at MSFT, and lack of this in the
&gt;&gt;&gt; Worker and Service Worker context is a constant bundle bloater.
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; Currently workers don't support anything related to the HTML parser.
&gt;&gt;&gt; e.g. It's impossible to create HTML DOM nodes in a worker. e.g.
&gt;&gt;&gt; `DOMParser` is only available in a `Window` context.
&gt;&gt;&gt; This means that the sanitizer is not particularly useful in workers in
&gt;&gt;&gt; isolation.
&gt;&gt;&gt; Either way, this feels a bit orthogonal (though worth exploring).
