&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;
&lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22Re%5C%3A+%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebTransport+congestion+control%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Re: [blink-dev] Intent to Ship: WebTransport congestion control&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt;
&lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22%27Monica+Chintala%27+via+blink%5C-dev%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;'Monica Chintala' via blink-dev&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20261019" rel="nofollow"&gt;Mon, 19 Oct 2026 15:08:27 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;

&lt;pre&gt;Adam, could you confirm whether this is appropriate to ship as-is? During 
the implementation review, BBRv1 was determined not to be suitable, so 
&quot;low-latency&quot; was mapped to BBRv2 for now, the same algorithm used for 
&quot;throughput&quot; with a TODO to revisit it.  &lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;

Given that background, do we consider BBRv2 to satisfy both preferences, or 
should &quot;low-latency&quot; fall back to &quot;default&quot; until a more suitable option is 
available? Your context on that decision would help address the concern.

On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 2:33:31 PM UTC-7 Reilly Grant wrote:

&gt; I'm concerned that shipping this is premature because according to the 
&gt; code 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:net/quic/dedicated_web_transport_http3_client.cc;l=472;drc=4e393f181c91e3b221ece53fb83205e3602b6495"&gt;https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:net/quic/dedicated_web_transport_http3_client.cc;l=472;drc=4e393f181c91e3b221ece53fb83205e3602b6495&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;  both 
&gt; the &quot;throughput&quot; and &quot;low-latency&quot; options apply the same congestion 
&gt; control algorithm, with a TODO to consider doing something else.
&gt; Reilly Grant | Software Engineer | &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="c7b5a2aee9e9e987a4afb5a8aaaeb2aae9a8b5a0"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; | Google Chrome 
&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.google.com/chrome"&gt;https://www.google.com/chrome&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;
&gt;
&gt; On Mon, Oct 19, 2026 at 12:10 PM 'Monica Chintala' via blink-dev &lt;
&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="0466686d6a2a2a2a44676c766b696d71692a6b7663"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;
&gt;&gt; Thank you all. I’ve updated both browser positions to Neutral and added 
&gt;&gt; clarifying comments per the recommendation, but the changes don’t appear to 
&gt;&gt; be showing on the ChromeStatus entry yet.
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt; On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 11:35:04 AM UTC-7 Dan Clark wrote:
&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; LGTM2
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt; On Monday, October 19, 2026 at 11:29:17 AM UTC-7 &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="fc8f90959b94d2d2d2bc9f948e9391958991d2938e9b"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Can we update the positions of other browsers to be more accurate 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; (e.g., neutral) given that they have not actually shipped the whole 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; feature?
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; With that nit, LGTM1.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; On Thursday, October 15, 2026 at 10:45:48 AM UTC-7 Monica Chintala 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; wrote:
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Thanks, the existing Mozilla and WebKit links are positions on Web 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Transport overall, not specifically on the congestion-control option. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Both 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Firefox and Safari expose the API surface, but their behavioral support 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; differs. Firefox currently ignores the requested value and reports the 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; spec-permitted  &quot;default&quot;  fallback. Safari reflects the requested value, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; but its open-source network-process implementation does not pass it into 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the QUIC configuration. The shared WPT passes in all three engines, but 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; it 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; intentionally permits fallback to &quot;default&quot; and therefore tests API 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; interoperability rather than the underlying congestion-control behavior.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; On Thursday, October 15, 2026 at 10:13:21 AM UTC-7 Mike Taylor wrote:
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; On 10/14/26 6:45 p.m., Chromestatus wrote:
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Contact emails*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="b1dcdedfd89f9f9ff1dcd8d2c3dec2ded7c59fd2dedc"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Explainer*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md"&gt;https://github.com/w3c/webtransport/blob/main/explainer.md&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Specification*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#dom-webtransportoptions-congestioncontrol"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#dom-webtransportoptions-congestioncontrol&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;  
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Summary*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Adds the congestionControl constructor option and read-only attribute 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; to WebTransport. Applications can request &quot;default&quot;, &quot;throughput&quot;, or 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &quot;low-latency&quot; behavior, allowing the user agent to select suitable 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; congestion-control behavior for each session. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Blink component*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Blink&gt;Network&gt;WebTransport 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues?q=customfield1222907:%22Blink%3ENetwork%3EWebTransport%22&lt;/a&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web Feature ID*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; webtransport &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport"&gt;https://webstatus.dev/features/webtransport&lt;/a&gt;&gt; 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Motivation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransport applications have different performance requirements. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Interactive applications prioritize low latency, while bulk transfers 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; prioritize throughput. This hint lets applications express that 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; preference 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; without selecting or controlling a specific congestion-control 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; algorithm. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Initial public proposal*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *No information provided*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Not applicable as a separate review. This is an incremental 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; implementation of an API already defined in the W3C WebTransport 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; specification and covered by the broader WebTransport TAG review: 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1212"&gt;https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1212&lt;/a&gt; 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *TAG review status*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Not applicable
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Goals for experimentation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Risks*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Interoperability and Compatibility*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. The API is additive, standardized, and already implemented by 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Firefox and Safari. Implementations may choose different underlying 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; algorithms while preserving the standardized hint and fallback behavior 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Gecko*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/167"&gt;https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/167&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebKit*: Shipped/Shipping (
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/18"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/18&lt;/a&gt;)
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Are they shipping congestion control, or just WebTransport? 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Web developers*: Positive (
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121"&gt;https://github.com/web-platform-tests/interop/issues/1121&lt;/a&gt;) The 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Interop 2026 WebTransport proposal received 28 👍 reactions. Developers 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; also reported active WebTransport use and significant cross-engine 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; differences.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Other signals*:
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Ergonomics*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. The three values are straightforward, but developers must 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; understand that the option is a hint and may resolve to &quot;default&quot; when 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; requested behavior is unavailable.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Activation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. This is an additive constructor option and attribute. The value 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; is a preference rather than a guarantee, and unsupported preferences may 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; fall back to &quot;default&quot;.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Security*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. The API grants no new network access and does not expose 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; congestion-window or network-state information. Applications provide 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; only a 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; high-level preference; the browser retains control of the selected 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; algorithm.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *WebView application risks*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; applications? 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Low. This is an additive WebTransport option and attribute 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; implemented in shared Blink and network-service code. Existing WebView 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; applications are unaffected, and applications using the option must 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; treat 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; it as a preference that may fall back to &quot;default&quot;. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Debuggability*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; The requested value is readable through 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;  transport.congestionControl . Existing DevTools networking, NetLog, and 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; QUIC logging can diagnose connection behavior 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Yes
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md"&gt;https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/main/docs/testing/web_platform_tests.md&lt;/a&gt;&gt;?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Yes 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/congestion-control.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned"&gt;https://wpt.fyi/results/webtransport/congestion-control.https.any.html?label=experimental&amp;label=master&amp;aligned&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;  
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Flag name on about://flags*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; chrome://flags/#enable-experimental-web-platform-features 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Finch feature name*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransportCongestionControl 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Rollout plan*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Will ship enabled for all users
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Requires code in //chrome?*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; False
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Tracking bug*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/501268547"&gt;https://issues.chromium.org/issues/501268547&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Measurement*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Validate API exposure and connection behavior through WPT, Chromium 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; tests, and developer feedback from latency-sensitive and high-throughput 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransport applications.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Availability expectation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Already supported by Firefox and Safari as part of the W3C 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransport specification. Chromium support is implemented behind an 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; experimental runtime feature.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Adoption expectation*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Expected to be adopted by games, interactive media, 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; remote-interaction applications, and bulk-transfer services that need to 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; express latency or throughput preferences.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Adoption plan*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Adoption is expected to happen organically through existing 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransport applications and documentation.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Estimated milestones*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on desktop 158 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTrial on desktop 150 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on Android 158 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; DevTrial on Android 150 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Shipping on WebView 158 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Anticipated spec changes*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; or interop issues. Please list open issues (e.g. links to known github 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; issues in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; may 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; introduce web compat/interop risk (e.g., changing to naming or structure 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; of 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; the API in a non-backward-compatible way). 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; None. The option and attribute are already defined by the W3C 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; WebTransport specification.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; *Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status*
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5180947573112832?gate=5529995941380096"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/5180947573112832?gate=5529995941380096&lt;/a&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; This intent message was generated by Chrome Platform Status 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; &lt;&lt;a  rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com"&gt;https://chromestatus.com&lt;/a&gt;&gt;. 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; -- 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google 
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Groups &quot;blink-dev&quot; group.
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;
