&lt;div class="msgHead"&gt; &lt;h1&gt; &lt;span class="subject"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=subject:%22%5C%5Bblink%5C-dev%5C%5D+Intent+to+Ship%5C%3A+WebGPU%5C%3A+atomic%5C-vec2u%5C-min%5C-max%22&amp;o=newest" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;[blink-dev] Intent to Ship: WebGPU: atomic-vec2u-min-max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/h1&gt; &lt;p class="darkgray font13"&gt; &lt;span class="sender pipe"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=from:%22Chromestatus%22" rel="nofollow"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/Person"&gt;&lt;span itemprop="name"&gt;Chromestatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="date"&gt;&lt;a href="/search?l=blink-dev@chromium.org&amp;q=date:20260921" rel="nofollow"&gt;Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:43:50 -0700&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div itemprop="articleBody" class="msgBody"&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;Contact emails &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="39534b494b171717795e56565e555c175a5654"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="90f4e3f9fef3fcbebebed0f7fffff7fcf5bef3fffd"&gt;[email&#160;protected]&lt;/a&gt; &lt;/pre&gt;&lt;pre&gt;
Explainer &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/issues/5071"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/issues/5071&lt;/a&gt;
Specification &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/#dom-gpufeaturename-atomic-vec2u-min-max"&gt;https://www.w3.org/TR/webgpu/#dom-gpufeaturename-atomic-vec2u-min-max&lt;/a&gt;
Summary Allows the use of 64-bit atomic minimum and maximum operations on `atomic&lt;vec2u&gt;` in WGSL. This provides WebGPU shader authors with a way to implement certain algorithms that use 64-bit atomics without requiring full support for 64-bit integers.
Blink component Blink&gt;WebGPU
Web Feature ID webgpu
Motivation This provides WebGPU shader authors with a way to implement certain algorithms that use 64-bit atomics without requiring full support for 64-bit integers. Not every device that supports 64-bit atomics can support every atomic operation on these types, so this feature is scoped to the min and max operations to deliver broadest reach.
Initial public proposal &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/blob/main/proposals/atomic-64-min-max.md"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/blob/main/proposals/atomic-64-min-max.md&lt;/a&gt;
TAG review No information provided
TAG review status Not applicable
Goals for experimentation None
Risks
Interoperability and Compatibility This feature has been approved in W3C GPU for the Web WG meetings including participants from Safari and Firefox. &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/wiki/GPU-Web-2026%E2%80%9009%E2%80%9015-WGSL#add-limited-support-for-64-bit-atomics-5071"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/wiki/GPU-Web-2026%E2%80%9009%E2%80%9015-WGSL#add-limited-support-for-64-bit-atomics-5071&lt;/a&gt;
Gecko: No signal (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/pull/5610"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/pull/5610&lt;/a&gt;) Feedback indicated via WebGPU standardization process.
WebKit: Positive (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/pull/5610"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/pull/5610&lt;/a&gt;) Specification approval constitutes a positive signal, per &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/294#issuecomment-1877411933"&gt;https://github.com/WebKit/standards-positions/issues/294#issuecomment-1877411933&lt;/a&gt;
Web developers: Positive (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/issues/5071#issuecomment-2660636760"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/gpuweb/issues/5071#issuecomment-2660636760&lt;/a&gt;)
Other signals:
WebView application risks
Does this intent deprecate or change behavior of existing APIs, such that it has potentially high risk for Android WebView-based applications? No information provided
Debuggability No information provided
Will this feature be supported on all six Blink platforms (Windows, Mac, Linux, ChromeOS, Android, and Android WebView)? Yes All platforms have support, where hardware support is available.
Is this feature fully tested by web-platform-tests? Yes WebGPU/WGSL have a conformance test suite (&lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/cts"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/cts&lt;/a&gt;) that is regularly pulled into Chromium and part of the testing of Dawn/Tint in Chromium. While the CTS can be embedded in WPT, the WebGPU team opted to keep it separate in Chromium testing to use a customized harness for robustness and performance. Tests added here: &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/gpuweb/cts/pull/4662"&gt;https://github.com/gpuweb/cts/pull/4662&lt;/a&gt;
Flag name on about://flags No information provided
Finch feature name WebGPU
Rollout plan Will ship enabled for all users
Requires code in //chrome? False
Tracking bug &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://crbug.com/453689550"&gt;https://crbug.com/453689550&lt;/a&gt;
Estimated milestones
Shipping on desktop 156
DevTrial on desktop 155
Shipping on Android 156
DevTrial on Android 155
Shipping on WebView 156
Anticipated spec changes
Open questions about a feature may be a source of future web compat or interop issues. Please list open issues (eg links to known github issues in the project for the feature specification) whose resolution may introduce web compat/interop risk (eg, changing to naming or structure of the API in a non-backward-compatible way). No information provided
Link to entry on the Chrome Platform Status &lt;a rel="nofollow" href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/4882016087703552?gate=5204891327922176"&gt;https://chromestatus.com/feature/4882016087703552?gate=5204891327922176&lt;/a&gt;
